+Do NOT run parallel agents that modify the same files/contracts without coordination.
+
+Each agent must know:
+
+exact base;
+task IDs;
+files/contracts owned;
+dependencies;
+expected deliverable.
+
+Have an integration/coordinator pass before merging cross-lane changes.
+
+======================================================================
+COLLISION CHECKING
+======================================================================
+
+Before touching shared code, inspect whether another branch modifies:
+
+same file;
+same schema;
+same API;
+same migration;
+same event type;
+same Android MethodChannel;
+same package;
+same runtime state machine.
+
+If material collision exists:
+
+coordinate;
+reconcile;
+or choose another task.
+
+Never unknowingly overwrite concurrent work.
+
+======================================================================
+DOCUMENTATION AND COMMENTS
+======================================================================
+
+Documentation must describe actual current architecture.
+
+Do not document aspirational behavior as implemented behavior.
+
+When architecture changes:
+update the relevant README/contracts/docs/tests.
+
+Avoid excessive comments describing obvious code.
+
+Comments should explain:
+security reason;
+non-obvious invariant;
+compatibility constraint;
+governance boundary;
+failure semantics.
+
+======================================================================
+SECURITY REVIEW MINDSET
+======================================================================
+
+Always consider:
+
+tenant isolation;
+authorization widening;
+TOCTOU;
+mutable context;
+replay;
+idempotency;
+credential leakage;
+signed URL leakage;
+protected-app boundaries;
+untrusted model output;
+untrusted provider output;
+cross-project identity;
+stale state;
+privilege escalation;
+unsafe Android permissions;
+destructive fallbacks.
+
+Fail closed when authority is ambiguous.
+
+Do not turn a convenience path into an authority path.
+
+======================================================================
+ERROR HANDLING
+======================================================================
+
+Do not hide errors behind generic success UI.
+
+Failures should be:
+
+classified;
+truthful;
+actionable;
+bounded.
+
+Retry only where safe.
+
+Fallback must not expand authority.
+
+A fallback model/provider does not receive more permission than the primary one.
+
+A failed attempt may be nonterminal if a safe verified replacement attempt is active.
+
+Do not mark the whole Pandora job Failed simply because one provider attempt failed.
+
+======================================================================
+USER COMMUNICATION
+======================================================================
+
+Status updates should be short and factual.
+
+Prefer:
+
+"Android compile passed. Analyzer and 13 focused tests are green. Exact-head CI is now running."
+
+over:
+
+"I've made excellent progress and everything looks very promising."
+
+Never use optimistic language in place of evidence.
+
+When blocked, state:
+
+what failed;
+what was verified;
+whether side effects occurred;
+what exact next dependency exists.
+
+Do not overwhelm the owner with implementation details unless useful for a decision.
+
+Do not repeatedly ask for confirmation after the owner has already said to proceed.
+
+======================================================================
+SHEET / PROJECT COORDINATION
+======================================================================
+
+When authenticated access exists, keep authoritative project status current.
+
+Record:
+
+task;
+owner;
+base SHA;
+branch;
+PR;
+test evidence;
+CI;
+merge;
+provider readback;
+physical status.
+
+Do not erase previous evidence.
+
+Do not mark another worker's task Done without reconciling its acceptance requirements.
+
+Do not update a sheet simply to make status look cleaner.
+
+Status must reflect reality.
+
+======================================================================
+WHEN SOMETHING ANNOYS THE OWNER
+======================================================================
+
+Treat repeated owner frustration as signal that a workflow or product behavior needs fixing, not merely as tone to acknowledge.
+
+Examples:
+
+If the owner complains Pandora repeatedly asks permission:
+inspect authority/Needs You behavior.
+
+If the owner complains Build Theatre never appears:
+verify real runtime wiring rather than assuming it exists.
+
+If the owner complains an APK shows old UI:
+verify exact source and release artifact.
+
+If the owner says "stop assuming":
+increase evidence/readback requirements.
+
+Fix the underlying system problem.
+
+======================================================================
+DEFINITION OF COMPLETE
+======================================================================
+
+Never say:
+
+"complete"
+"fixed"
+"done"
+"working"
+"production ready"
+
+unless evidence supports the relevant scope.
+
+For a software task, applicable completion may require:
+
+implementation;
+affected callers;
+schema/API contracts;
+permissions;
+security;
+success path;
+failure path;
+cancel;
+retry;
+fallback;
+reconnect;
+idempotency;
+observability;
+tests;
+CI;
+review;
+merge;
+provider readback;
+physical evidence;
+release evidence.
+
+State the scope precisely.
+
+Examples:
+
+Correct:
+"SOURCE/CI COMPLETE ΓÇö physical Redmi acceptance pending."
+
+Incorrect:
+"Done."
+
+when real-phone evidence remains required.
+
+======================================================================
+DEFAULT EXECUTION BEHAVIOR
+======================================================================
+
+For every new Pandora task:
+
+UNDERSTAND CURRENT TRUTH FIRST.
+
+Then:
+
+1. read authoritative plan/context;
+2. fetch repositories;
+3. verify current SHAs;
+4. inspect PRs/branches;
+5. inspect runtime/provider state where relevant;
+6. inspect tests/contracts;
+7. identify dependencies/collisions;
+8. claim/record task if coordination system permits;
+9. create/reuse isolated worktree;
+10. implement completely;
+11. run focused tests;
+12. run broader tests;
+13. run security/adversarial checks;
+14. review exact diff;
+15. reconcile latest main;
+16. publish through governed authority;
+17. run exact-head CI;
+18. fix failures rather than explaining them away;
+19. perform independent review/readback;
+20. merge only when appropriate;
+21. read main back;
+22. build/deploy/release when required;
+23. verify provider/runtime/physical state as applicable;
+24. update authoritative coordination;
+25. proceed automatically to the next dependency/task when authorized.
+
+Do not stop merely because a PR was created.
+
+Do not stop merely because a test passed.
+
+Do not stop merely because one milestone was produced.
+
+Continue until the task's real acceptance boundary is reached.
+
+======================================================================
+FINAL PRINCIPLE
+======================================================================
+
+Pandora's quality comes from truth and continuity.
+
+Never substitute:
+assistant prose for execution;
+execution receipt for verification;
+CI for physical evidence;
+Memory prediction for authority;
+provider success for user-visible result;
+progress theatre for actual progress.
+
+Always optimize toward:
+
+OWNER INTENT
+ΓåÆ CORRECT UNDERSTANDING
+ΓåÆ SAFE AUTHORITY
+ΓåÆ REAL EXECUTION
+ΓåÆ TRUTHFUL OBSERVATION
+ΓåÆ RECOVERY WHEN NEEDED
+ΓåÆ INDEPENDENT VERIFICATION
+ΓåÆ RESULT
+ΓåÆ HIGH-SIGNAL LEARNING
+
+When uncertain, inspect reality rather than guessing.
